# Email Guard -- copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is git-ignored.
#
# Compose reads this file automatically. Everything marked REQUIRED has no
# usable default and compose will refuse to start without it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: this repository's absolute path ON THE HOST.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The dispatcher asks the host's docker daemon to bind directories into each
# scanner container, and the daemon resolves those paths on the host -- not
# inside the dispatcher's filesystem. This is how the dispatcher learns what
# its own mounts are called from outside.
#
# It must be absolute, and it must be the real path on the machine running the
# daemon. `pwd` in this directory, on that machine.
#
# Getting it wrong fails quietly: the scanner container starts, finds nothing
# where the rules pack should be, and reports a rules-pack error. See
# VALIDATION.md.
EMAIL_GUARD_HOST_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/email-guard

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: the EXISTING, ALREADY-LOGGED-IN Proton Bridge config directory.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This stack reuses the bridge that is already set up rather than initialising
# a new one -- pointing this at an empty directory forces the whole account
# login and keychain dance again.
#
# For shenxn/protonmail-bridge this is whatever is currently mounted at /root
# in the running bridge container. Confirm with:
#     docker inspect -f '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Source}} -> {{.Destination}}{{"\n"}}{{end}}' <bridge-container>
EMAIL_GUARD_BRIDGE_CONFIG_DIR=/absolute/path/to/protonmail-bridge-config

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED: the BRIDGE-GENERATED IMAP credentials.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not the Proton account password. Find them in the Bridge app under the
# account's "Mailbox details" / "Configure".
EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_USERNAME=you@proton.me
EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_PASSWORD=the-bridge-generated-password

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connection to the bridge -- these defaults are correct for compose. Read this
# before changing either of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PORT: 143, not 1143. Both numbers are real and they mean different things.
# 1143 is what the bridge listens on inside its own container, and what it
# publishes to the host when run standalone -- which is why every Proton Bridge
# document quotes it. The shenxn image runs socat in front, exposing 143 on the
# container network and forwarding to the bridge's 1143. The dispatcher reaches
# the bridge over the container network, so it wants 143. Using 1143 here gets
# a flat connection refusal.
# EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_PORT=143
#
# TLS: `none`, and deliberately. The dispatcher<->bridge hop runs on a compose
# network declared `internal: true` -- no gateway, no route off this host, and
# nothing attached to it but those two containers. That is the same reasoning
# that lets the dispatcher accept the bridge's self-signed certificate over
# loopback.
#
# It is also not a choice between this and something better, today: an
# encrypted hop is not currently reachable. The bridge's certificate is issued
# for `localhost` / `127.0.0.1`, so verifying it against the service name
# `bridge` fails on hostname mismatch whatever CA file is supplied -- and there
# is deliberately no verify-off switch, because the dispatcher refuses to
# silently downgrade verification for a non-loopback host. Closing that needs a
# TLS mode meaning "encrypt, trust this certificate, skip the hostname check".
# See TODO(bridge-tls) in docker-compose.yml.
# EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_TLS=none
# EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_CA_FILE=

# Which mailbox to watch. Point the first live run at a test intake mailbox.
# EMAIL_GUARD_IMAP_MAILBOX=INBOX

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run the services as your own user, so the lists and reports they write stay
# editable by hand on the host. `id -u` and `id -g`.
# A dispatcher running as root is refused: its uid becomes the scanner
# container's uid, and those must not be root.
EMAIL_GUARD_UID=1000
EMAIL_GUARD_GID=1000

# Which HOST port the review console is published on. This only moves it out of
# the way of whatever else already has 8080; the container still listens on 8080
# internally. Which INTERFACE it is published on is the next variable, and that
# is the one that matters.
# EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_HOST_PORT=8080

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which interface the review console is published on. READ THIS ONE.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default `127.0.0.1` -- LOOPBACK ONLY, reachable from this host and nothing
# else. Leaving it unset, or having no `.env` at all, keeps that default: the
# console is never exposed by accident.
#
# Set it to `0.0.0.0` to publish on the LAN, and understand what that hands out.
# The console reads whole messages, shows real senders, and edits the lists that
# decide what mail is delivered -- over plain HTTP, with no password unless you
# configure one. On `0.0.0.0`, everything on the network segment can reach it.
#
#   EXPOSING IT ON THE LAN REQUIRES EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_TOKEN BELOW.
#
# That is ENFORCED, not merely advised. Set this to a non-loopback address with
# an empty token and the console refuses to start -- it exits 3 with a message
# saying so, and `docker compose up` leaves the webui container in a restart
# loop rather than serving mail to the LAN unauthenticated. Set the token in
# the same edit.
#
# (Compose passes the address in as EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_PUBLISHED_BIND, which is
# the one thing the container cannot work out for itself -- inside its own
# namespace it binds 0.0.0.0 either way. You do not set that variable; it is
# derived from this one.)
#
# NEVER put the console on the public internet, and that includes a tunnel --
# Cloudflare Tunnel, ngrok, a reverse proxy on a public hostname, a forwarded
# port on the router. A shared token is a reasonable answer to "other people on
# my LAN"; it is not a reasonable answer to the internet, and this console has
# no rate limiting, no lockout, no TLS of its own, and full authority over the
# mailbox behind it. If you need it from outside, use a VPN into the LAN.
#
# (This variable exists because the alternative was worse: the one deploy that
# wanted LAN access hand-edited the tracked docker-compose.yml, which then
# collided with every `git pull`. Configuration belongs here.)
# EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Require a shared token on every review-console API call. Worth setting on a
# shared machine, where "loopback" includes every other user on it -- and
# REQUIRED if EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_BIND is anything other than 127.0.0.1, where
# the console refuses to start without it.
#
#     EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# It can also live in config/secrets.json under `webui`.`token` (git-ignored,
# like the bridge credentials); either satisfies the startup check.
# EMAIL_GUARD_WEBUI_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: where each verdict is POSTed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave it unset and no webhook sink is built at all -- the dispatcher logs each
# verdict and files it, and stays egress-free. Setting it is what wires the
# scanner's output into a downstream automation (n8n, Home Assistant, whatever
# consumes the JSON).
#
# The URL to use depends on where that downstream runs, and the working answer
# is usually not the address you'd type into a browser:
#
#   (a) IT RUNS ON THIS HOST -- the common case, e.g. n8n in its own compose
#       stack. Reach it over ITS docker network, by service name, on its
#       INTERNAL port:
#
#           EMAIL_GUARD_WEBHOOK_URL=http://n8n:5678/webhook/email-guard
#
#       and attach the dispatcher to that network with an override file (README,
#       "Optional webhook"). Using the public hostname instead sends the POST
#       out of the host and back in through the edge, where a bot filter can
#       simply refuse it -- Cloudflare returns `error 1010` to a bare
#       container-issued POST, and the delivery is lost with a 403-shaped log
#       line that says nothing about why.
#
#   (b) IT RUNS SOMEWHERE ELSE -- a public URL, and the dispatcher then needs
#       the `egress` network added in an override too. It is attached to `mail`
#       and `dockerproxy` only, so an off-host URL without that fails to
#       resolve rather than failing to authenticate.
#
# Also used by the onboarding pass: `--rescan --emit-webhooks` replays the whole
# recognised, tagged backlog into this URL. A plain `--rescan` fires nothing.
# EMAIL_GUARD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: the rules pack auto-updater.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The `rules-updater` service pulls the rules pack from git, validates it, and
# swaps a symlink so the NEXT scanner container mounts the new tree. Nothing
# restarts: each message is scanned by a fresh `docker run --rm`, so no
# long-lived process is holding the old rules.
#
# The defaults below are the intended operating mode and need no configuration
# at all: pull this repository's `rules/` subtree from `main`, once a day.
#
# AUTHENTICATION: none, and not by omission. The updater pulls PUBLIC
# repositories over HTTPS and never supplies credentials -- it does not read
# tokens, SSH keys or git credential helpers from the environment, and actively
# scrubs them from the environment it hands to git. A private repository is
# refused at startup with a message saying so, rather than hanging on a password
# prompt in a container with no terminal. Configuring an ssh:// or git@host:path
# URL is a startup error, not a fallback.
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_REPO_URL=https://github.com/Raven-git-hub/email-guard
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_BRANCH=main
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_SUBPATH=rules
#
# How often to pull. Accepts `off`, `hourly`, `daily`, `weekly`, or a number
# with a unit (`24h`, `7d`, `90m`). `off` disables the schedule and leaves the
# console's "Refresh rules" button working -- useful when you would rather
# decide when detection logic changes.
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_PULL_INTERVAL=24h
#
# How many promoted releases to keep on disk. Old ones are pruned; the live one,
# the seed, and anything promoted in the last hour are never touched (a scan
# container that started moments ago may still have it mounted).
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_KEEP_RELEASES=3
#
# Shared token for the console -> updater hop. That hop runs on a compose
# network declared `internal: true` with two containers on it and no route off
# this host, so it is defensible empty -- but one line here feeds both sides, so
# the two can never skew, and setting it costs nothing.
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_CONTROL_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: cut the scanner over to the auto-updated rules tree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS IS A DEPLOY-TIME CHANGE, NOT JUST CODE. Until these two are set, the
# updater still runs and still maintains `rules-live/` -- but the scanner keeps
# reading the committed `./rules`, so a pull changes nothing that scans. That is
# deliberate: it lets you watch the updater promote a few commits, confirm the
# tree is good, and only then point the scanner at it.
#
# Set BOTH, together:
#
#     EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_LIVE_NAME=rules-live
#     EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_LIVE_POINTER=/current
#
# then `docker compose up -d dispatcher` to recreate it against the new mount.
# VALIDATION.md, "Cut over to the managed rules tree", has the ordering and the
# checks that prove it took effect.
#
#   default   NAME=rules       POINTER=          -> ${EMAIL_GUARD_HOST_ROOT}/rules
#   cut over  NAME=rules-live  POINTER=/current  -> ${EMAIL_GUARD_HOST_ROOT}/rules-live/current
#
# WHY TWO VARIABLES rather than one path. The dispatcher binds the DIRECTORY
# (`rules-live/`) and then looks at `current` INSIDE it. Binding the symlink
# itself would make the docker daemon resolve it once, at container start, and
# mount the release it pointed at then -- the dispatcher would be pinned to that
# release forever and would never see a promote. NAME picks the directory to
# bind; POINTER picks the path within it. Each scanner container is the opposite
# case and is handled for you: it is created fresh per message, so resolving the
# symlink at create time is exactly right -- it gets one immutable release for
# its whole life, and a swap mid-scan cannot affect it.
#
# Run the updater once BEFORE cutting over, so `rules-live/current` exists:
#
#     docker compose run --rm rules-updater python -m email_guard_rules_sync --once
#
# Cutting over against an empty `rules-live/` fails loudly rather than quietly --
# the dispatcher refuses to start on a rules directory that is not there.
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_LIVE_NAME=rules
# EMAIL_GUARD_RULES_LIVE_POINTER=
